JUDGE REPORT   (4000 held-out inputs; construction 1,651 chars)
1. BEHAVIOUR  construction vs network output: same decision on 92.9% of inputs (a constant output would get 26.6%); output R2 0.973; KL 0.019
2-3. SITES   explained = share of the activation's variance the variables explain;  substitute = network decision kept when the activation is replaced by the construction's prediction;  keep = when only the named subspace of the real activation is kept;  remove = when the named subspace is removed (should be LOW) vs a random subspace of equal size;  mean-ablate = when the whole site is replaced by its mean
  h1          (2 dims named of 4; 2 principal components suffice; h1): explained 1.000 | substitute 100.0% | keep 100.0% | remove 51.5% (random 45.3%) | mean-ablate 51.5%
  h2          (4 dims named of 4; 4 principal components suffice; h2): explained 1.000 | substitute 100.0% | keep 100.0% | remove 41.2% (random n/a) | mean-ablate 41.2%
  h3          (4 dims named of 4; 4 principal components suffice; h3): explained 1.000 | substitute 100.0% | keep 100.0% | remove 32.4% (random n/a) | mean-ablate 32.4%
  h4          (4 dims named of 4; 4 principal components suffice; h4): explained 1.000 | substitute 100.0% | keep 100.0% | remove 26.6% (random n/a) | mean-ablate 26.6%
4. INTERVENTIONS   interchange = everything named at the site is copied from another input's run (need 90%). Then tests that make states no input produces (need 50% of the cases where the decision changed): partial = one variable is copied while the site's other variables stay; edit = a random half of the coordinates of everything named at the site are copied. Where both are run, it is enough that all partial tests pass OR the edit passes. right = the construction predicted the network's new decision (all cases / cases where the decision changed).  controls: the same swap in a random subspace; guessing 'no change'; guessing the source input's output; attribution patching (a first-order estimate computed on the network itself)
